1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,944
Here is a cheese chatbot,
running on your laptop.

2
00:00:03,264 --> 00:00:05,461
You ask it how Tolminc cheese is made.

3
00:00:05,781 --> 00:00:09,536
An answer appears,
with ten book passages as references.

4
00:00:09,783 --> 00:00:13,004
Behind that one answer,
your browser makes three calls.

5
00:00:13,324 --> 00:00:19,767
Two go to a Python server on port 9000,
and one goes to a database on port 8000.

6
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:23,458
Behind both server calls,
Google does the heavy lifting.

7
00:00:23,778 --> 00:00:25,655
So who checks that it is really you?

8
00:00:25,975 --> 00:00:29,794
And how does a web page find
a database inside a container?

9
00:00:30,033 --> 00:00:32,038
Three small riddles hide in this app.

10
00:00:32,358 --> 00:00:37,862
The flag -p 3200:3000 has two numbers; why?

11
00:00:38,182 --> 00:00:41,446
And localhost:9000: which machine is that?

12
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:45,692
And llm-rag-chromadb is only a name;
where is its address?

13
00:00:46,012 --> 00:00:47,910
Each one gets its answer today.

14
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:52,817
Then the real question arrives:
once anything can knock, who is allowed in?

15
00:00:53,067 --> 00:00:56,542
To follow
along you need your clone of cheese-app-v0,

16
00:00:56,542 --> 00:01:00,214
Docker Desktop,
and a Google Cloud project with billing.

17
00:01:00,534 --> 00:01:04,069
This lecture makes real,
paid calls to Vertex AI;

18
00:01:04,069 --> 00:01:05,971
a gold tag marks each one,

19
00:01:05,971 --> 00:01:09,408
and the video ends
by shutting everything down.

20
00:01:09,728 --> 00:01:13,602
The tracker in the top left
follows the README in six stages,

21
00:01:13,602 --> 00:01:15,062
from setup to shutdown.

22
00:01:15,382 --> 00:01:18,372
A yellow your-turn tag
means pause and run it,

23
00:01:18,372 --> 00:01:22,336
and a gray extra tag marks a check
that is not in the README.

24
00:01:22,656 --> 00:01:25,664
Every command is in the study guide,
linked below.

25
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:29,473
First, the map,
which stays with us all lecture.

26
00:01:29,793 --> 00:01:31,435
Your laptop is one house,

27
00:01:31,435 --> 00:01:34,391
and every house
has its own loopback address,

28
00:01:34,391 --> 00:01:37,175
localhost, which always means this machine.

29
00:01:37,495 --> 00:01:41,164
Docker lays two private streets inside it,
one per network.

30
00:01:41,484 --> 00:01:43,761
Three services will stand on them:

31
00:01:43,761 --> 00:01:48,716
the vector database with its loader,
the API server, and the web frontend.

32
00:01:49,036 --> 00:01:51,596
Each house lights up
when its container starts.

33
00:01:51,833 --> 00:01:55,609
Your browser stands on the laptop floor,
outside every street.

34
00:01:55,929 --> 00:01:59,940
Across the public street sits
Google's building, with a guarded door.

35
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:01,646
Now, who calls whom?

36
00:02:01,966 --> 00:02:04,313
The loader calls Google and the database.

37
00:02:04,633 --> 00:02:06,382
The API calls Google.

38
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:08,644
Your browser calls everything else.

39
00:02:08,964 --> 00:02:13,529
And the API never talks to the database;
the README says so itself.

40
00:02:13,849 --> 00:02:16,431
Part 2 shows why the browser does so much.

41
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:17,776
Time to type.

42
00:02:18,096 --> 00:02:20,574
The README's prerequisites are short:

43
00:02:20,574 --> 00:02:23,856
have Docker installed,
and clone this repository.

44
00:02:24,176 --> 00:02:27,803
The README gives no address,
so here is the repo's address.

45
00:02:28,123 --> 00:02:29,467
Pause, and clone it.

46
00:02:32,633 --> 00:02:36,852
An extra git log shows
the commit this video uses,

47
00:02:36,852 --> 00:02:38,793
from October 8th, 2026;

48
00:02:38,793 --> 00:02:41,956
if yours is newer, details may differ.

49
00:02:42,276 --> 00:02:45,665
An extra listing shows llm-rag, api-service,

50
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:49,209
and frontend-ai-chatbot:
one folder per house.

51
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:50,602
One small gap.

52
00:02:50,922 --> 00:02:54,110
The app's own home page
animates a hosted version,

53
00:02:54,110 --> 00:02:56,469
with a web server on a cloud machine.

54
00:02:56,789 --> 00:02:59,903
The files for that setup
were deleted from the repo.

55
00:03:00,223 --> 00:03:04,490
You are running the local version,
and this map draws exactly what you run.

56
00:03:05,533 --> 00:03:09,309
The second prerequisite creates an identity,
but not for you.

57
00:03:09,629 --> 00:03:12,509
A service account
is an identity for software.

58
00:03:12,829 --> 00:03:17,437
Its email looks like this badge: llm-
service-account@your-gcp-
project.iam.gserviceaccount.com.

59
00:03:17,757 --> 00:03:21,128
Code wears it to call Google,
with no login screen.

60
00:03:22,016 --> 00:03:24,981
A metered public call gets an API key.

61
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:28,402
An app acting
for a consenting user gets OAuth,

62
00:03:28,402 --> 00:03:30,315
the Sign in with Google flow.

63
00:03:30,635 --> 00:03:33,528
Our code calls Vertex AI
in your own project,

64
00:03:33,528 --> 00:03:35,392
so it gets a service account.

65
00:03:35,712 --> 00:03:40,149
The rule's last line says
code outside Google should use federation.

66
00:03:40,469 --> 00:03:45,717
That needs someone to vouch for your code,
as GitHub does for code in GitHub Actions.

67
00:03:46,037 --> 00:03:50,006
A laptop has nobody,
so the course hands you a key file instead.

68
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:53,429
Open IAM & Admin, then Service accounts.

69
00:03:53,749 --> 00:03:56,266
Create one named llm-service-account.

70
00:03:56,586 --> 00:03:59,705
Grant it two roles, bundles of permissions:

71
00:03:59,705 --> 00:04:03,839
Storage Admin,
and Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform User.

72
00:04:04,159 --> 00:04:07,596
The second role
was formerly called Vertex AI User;

73
00:04:07,596 --> 00:04:09,685
it is the same role underneath.

74
00:04:10,005 --> 00:04:11,519
Pause and create yours.

75
00:04:11,766 --> 00:04:13,281
Two notes on the slides.

76
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:17,547
The slides' demo account,
with one storage role, is next week's.

77
00:04:17,867 --> 00:04:19,977
And this app never touches storage;

78
00:04:19,977 --> 00:04:23,051
that role rides
along from the fine-tuning lecture.

79
00:04:23,371 --> 00:04:25,910
Grant both anyway, as the README says.

80
00:04:26,230 --> 00:04:31,880
The lecture's rule, least privilege, meaning
only the access you need, would grant less;

81
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:33,420
part 2 comes back to it.

82
00:04:33,666 --> 00:04:38,658
On the account's Keys tab, add a key,
choose JSON, and a file downloads.

83
00:04:38,978 --> 00:04:40,386
Pause and make yours.

84
00:04:40,706 --> 00:04:42,133
Inside are your project,

85
00:04:42,133 --> 00:04:45,463
the account's email,
a token address, and a private key.

86
00:04:45,783 --> 00:04:48,087
That private key is the whole secret.

87
00:04:48,407 --> 00:04:50,875
It is one factor, something you have:

88
00:04:50,875 --> 00:04:55,277
it works anywhere, with no second step,
until deleted or disabled.

89
00:04:55,597 --> 00:04:58,754
Treat this file like a password,
never like config.

90
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:03,043
The README says to copy the key
into a secrets folder.

91
00:05:03,363 --> 00:05:04,363
Which one?

92
00:05:04,557 --> 00:05:07,181
The API's script answers on line 8.

93
00:05:07,501 --> 00:05:10,637
The path starts where you run it,
in api-service.

94
00:05:10,957 --> 00:05:14,221
It climbs up twice
and lands on the secrets folder,

95
00:05:14,221 --> 00:05:16,205
beside the repo, not inside it.

96
00:05:16,450 --> 00:05:21,975
Then a -v flag mounts that host folder
at /secrets, inside the container.

97
00:05:22,295 --> 00:05:27,337
The GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS variable,
which tells Google's library

98
00:05:27,337 --> 00:05:31,895
where the key is, points to /secrets,
a path inside the container.

99
00:05:32,215 --> 00:05:34,068
Two filesystems, one folder,

100
00:05:34,068 --> 00:05:37,442
and that variable only makes
sense after the mount.

101
00:05:37,683 --> 00:05:42,175
The loader's compose file builds
the same portal from its own folder:

102
00:05:42,175 --> 00:05:43,998
up two levels, same secrets.

103
00:05:44,318 --> 00:05:49,374
The lecture's slide climbs only once,
because its repos sit one level shallower.

104
00:05:49,694 --> 00:05:52,403
Always count the climbs
from where the script sits.

105
00:05:52,650 --> 00:05:55,971
Make the folder beside your clone,
as an extra,

106
00:05:55,971 --> 00:06:00,565
then move the download
in and rename it llm-service-account.json.

107
00:06:00,885 --> 00:06:05,557
Check that name:
hidden extensions can leave .json.json.

108
00:06:05,877 --> 00:06:08,735
An extra tree view shows
the README's layout.

109
00:06:09,055 --> 00:06:10,293
Pause and match it.

110
00:06:16,917 --> 00:06:18,105
Now a quiet trap:

111
00:06:18,105 --> 00:06:21,248
the repo's gitignore
has no rule for secrets.

112
00:06:21,568 --> 00:06:24,191
Nothing protects a key inside the repo.

113
00:06:24,433 --> 00:06:25,777
Something to ponder.

114
00:06:26,097 --> 00:06:29,408
Suppose you save the key inside the repo,

115
00:06:29,408 --> 00:06:34,737
in cheese-app-v0/secrets,
and run the API script from api-service.

116
00:06:35,057 --> 00:06:36,344
What is in /secrets,

117
00:06:36,344 --> 00:06:39,239
and what happens
on the first embedding call?

118
00:06:48,083 --> 00:06:52,072
The cursor still climbs twice,
to a folder that does not exist.

119
00:06:52,392 --> 00:06:56,744
Docker does not complain;
it silently creates that folder, empty.

120
00:06:57,064 --> 00:06:58,614
So /secrets is empty,

121
00:06:58,614 --> 00:07:03,488
and the first embedding call fails
with a DefaultCredentialsError:

122
00:07:03,488 --> 00:07:04,595
file not found.

123
00:07:04,915 --> 00:07:08,499
Meanwhile your key sits
in a git tree that nothing protects.

124
00:07:09,533 --> 00:07:12,200
Last prerequisite: the Docker network.

125
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:17,298
The README's command inspects it quietly,
and creates it only if that fails.

126
00:07:17,618 --> 00:07:18,770
Pause and run it.

127
00:07:19,016 --> 00:07:21,747
What happens
if you run it again, as an extra?

128
00:07:22,067 --> 00:07:24,669
Nothing: no error, no second network.

129
00:07:24,989 --> 00:07:28,616
A command that is safe
to run twice is called idempotent.

130
00:07:28,936 --> 00:07:30,152
Remember this shape.

131
00:07:30,433 --> 00:07:35,361
In llm-rag, the shell script ships
with a default project that is not yours.

132
00:07:35,681 --> 00:07:39,634
Update the GCP_PROJECT variable
to your own project ID;

133
00:07:39,634 --> 00:07:42,508
we use the sed tool, you use any editor.

134
00:07:42,828 --> 00:07:44,086
Pause and edit it.

135
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:48,419
Now the README's command:
sh docker-shell.sh.

136
00:07:48,739 --> 00:07:49,891
Pause and run it.

137
00:07:50,211 --> 00:07:54,402
Compose pulls Chroma,
creates a network called llm-rag-network,

138
00:07:54,402 --> 00:07:56,397
and starts the database on it.

139
00:07:56,717 --> 00:07:59,811
Every service in this file
joins it automatically.

140
00:08:00,131 --> 00:08:03,446
The line that says
pull access denied is harmless:

141
00:08:03,446 --> 00:08:07,555
that image is not on Docker Hub,
so Compose builds it locally.

142
00:08:07,875 --> 00:08:13,805
The container's architecture is x86 here;
Apple Silicon says aarch64.

143
00:08:14,050 --> 00:08:15,842
Two extra checks inside.

144
00:08:16,162 --> 00:08:19,575
The key file shows up in /secrets,
through the portal.

145
00:08:19,895 --> 00:08:24,233
And both variables are set,
copied by Compose from the launching script,

146
00:08:24,233 --> 00:08:26,402
which is why you always start there.

147
00:08:26,650 --> 00:08:28,954
One honest note about this recording.

148
00:08:29,274 --> 00:08:31,471
This machine has no Google account.

149
00:08:31,791 --> 00:08:35,653
The key file you just saw
is an obviously invalid stand-in.

150
00:08:35,973 --> 00:08:39,030
It is there so the folder
listings match the README,

151
00:08:39,030 --> 00:08:41,498
and it was removed before the cloud steps.

152
00:08:41,818 --> 00:08:46,746
So from here on, every cloud call fails
exactly the way a missing key fails.

153
00:08:47,066 --> 00:08:49,732
It is the most common
mistake in this lecture,

154
00:08:49,732 --> 00:08:50,949
so learn it on sight.

155
00:08:51,269 --> 00:08:55,194
Every success you should expect
is tagged recorded, with its source.

156
00:08:56,233 --> 00:09:00,469
Inside the container,
the README's first command does three jobs:

157
00:09:00,469 --> 00:09:03,401
it chunks, embeds and loads,
with char-split.

158
00:09:03,721 --> 00:09:06,324
The ID in the prompt changes every run.

159
00:09:06,644 --> 00:09:11,977
Chunking runs for real: 11 books,
and the Tolminc book becomes 24 chunks.

160
00:09:12,216 --> 00:09:13,560
A gold tag first.

161
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:17,955
The embed step sends every chunk
to Google's embedding model,

162
00:09:17,955 --> 00:09:22,029
about 7 million characters across both runs,
by our estimate.

163
00:09:22,349 --> 00:09:25,635
That bills your project;
check the current pricing page.

164
00:09:25,883 --> 00:09:27,086
The embed step stops

165
00:09:27,086 --> 00:09:29,851
while building the header
named Authorization.

166
00:09:30,171 --> 00:09:33,041
Despite the name,
it carries proof of who you are,

167
00:09:33,041 --> 00:09:34,992
a bearer token, which needs a key.

168
00:09:35,312 --> 00:09:38,811
DefaultCredentialsError:
the key file was not found.

169
00:09:39,131 --> 00:09:40,401
No key, no token:

170
00:09:40,401 --> 00:09:43,910
the first cloud request dies
at authentication.

171
00:09:44,230 --> 00:09:46,000
The next scene builds that chain.

172
00:09:46,250 --> 00:09:50,067
With a real key, the run continues:
it embeds every book,

173
00:09:50,067 --> 00:09:53,951
creates the char-split-collection,
and finishes inserting.

174
00:09:54,271 --> 00:09:57,173
Pause and run it;
with your key, it takes a while.

175
00:09:57,493 --> 00:10:04,240
If instead you see a 403 saying the API
is disabled, enable the Vertex AI API;

176
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:07,007
the IAM scene shows the command.

177
00:10:07,250 --> 00:10:10,578
The second command does the same
with recursive-split.

178
00:10:10,898 --> 00:10:13,138
Pause and run it, then type exit.

179
00:10:13,458 --> 00:10:18,286
An extra count
shows 8,248 chunks for char-split

180
00:10:18,286 --> 00:10:24,018
and 14,228 for recursive-split,
at the time of recording.

181
00:10:24,338 --> 00:10:26,638
Recursive split makes more chunks,

182
00:10:26,638 --> 00:10:30,290
mostly from a default overlap of up
to 200 characters,

183
00:10:30,290 --> 00:10:33,063
and it cuts at paragraph and word breaks.

184
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:36,564
The chunk files land on your laptop
through the app mount.

185
00:10:36,884 --> 00:10:38,484
One small slip shows here.

186
00:10:38,804 --> 00:10:42,928
A book title is cut at its first dot,
so its chunks get no author,

187
00:10:42,928 --> 00:10:45,865
and the agent's author
filter never finds them.

188
00:10:46,185 --> 00:10:48,809
The fix is a Python function, splitext.

189
00:10:49,129 --> 00:10:51,178
Last, one real Tolminc chunk:

190
00:10:51,178 --> 00:10:54,356
the kind of passage the chat hands
to Gemini.

191
00:10:55,800 --> 00:11:00,095
The README says ChromaDB
keeps running after you exit,

192
00:11:00,095 --> 00:11:03,992
and an extra docker ps confirms it,
on port 8000.

193
00:11:04,312 --> 00:11:10,435
An extra curl asks for its version:
1.0.0, at the time of recording.

194
00:11:10,755 --> 00:11:12,653
Stopping it comes at the very end.

195
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:16,652
So what failed?

196
00:11:16,972 --> 00:11:18,784
The loader's Google client,

197
00:11:18,784 --> 00:11:23,415
like the API's, is built from a project
and a location, nothing else.

198
00:11:23,735 --> 00:11:26,883
No code in this repo opens the key file;

199
00:11:26,883 --> 00:11:33,100
a library does, by a convention called
application default credentials, or ADC.

200
00:11:33,333 --> 00:11:35,936
ADC looks in three places, in order.

201
00:11:36,256 --> 00:11:40,234
First, the file named
by the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS

202
00:11:40,234 --> 00:11:43,808
variable: a service account key,
as in this tutorial.

203
00:11:44,128 --> 00:11:48,842
Second, a login file the gcloud
tool writes for you, as a person.

204
00:11:49,162 --> 00:11:53,706
Third, the metadata server,
when your code runs inside Google's cloud.

205
00:11:54,026 --> 00:11:58,400
ADC moves to the next place
only when the one before is not set.

206
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:02,240
Inside these containers,
only the first place exists.

207
00:12:02,483 --> 00:12:05,871
The library writes a small note,
a JSON Web Token,

208
00:12:05,871 --> 00:12:08,243
and signs it with your private key.

209
00:12:08,563 --> 00:12:13,000
It sends that note to Google's token desk,
at the address in your key file.

210
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:15,469
Google holds the matching public key,

211
00:12:15,469 --> 00:12:19,187
so it checks your signature
without ever seeing the private one.

212
00:12:19,507 --> 00:12:22,878
Back comes an access token,
valid for about an hour.

213
00:12:23,198 --> 00:12:26,526
That token rides on every request
as a bearer header.

214
00:12:26,846 --> 00:12:31,646
Your private key never leaves your machine;
only the short-lived ticket travels.

215
00:12:31,883 --> 00:12:34,848
When the hour runs out,
the library gets a new one.

216
00:12:35,168 --> 00:12:38,716
The lecture's OAuth diagram ends
at this same desk,

217
00:12:38,716 --> 00:12:41,846
minus the consent screen:
no person is there.

218
00:12:42,083 --> 00:12:44,152
Here is ADC caught in the act.

219
00:12:44,472 --> 00:12:46,149
With /secrets empty,

220
00:12:46,149 --> 00:12:50,424
an extra one-line Python check asks ADC
who we are.

221
00:12:50,744 --> 00:12:53,772
The variable is set, so ADC opens that path,

222
00:12:53,772 --> 00:12:57,763
finds no file, and stops right there,
with the same error.

223
00:12:58,083 --> 00:13:01,390
A wrong path never falls back
to the other two places.

224
00:13:01,710 --> 00:13:05,209
With your key in place,
it names your service account instead.

225
00:13:05,450 --> 00:13:06,644
One common mix-up:

226
00:13:06,644 --> 00:13:11,618
setting that variable on your Mac
authenticates nothing inside a container,

227
00:13:11,618 --> 00:13:13,343
where the path must exist.

228
00:13:13,663 --> 00:13:16,204
That is why the scripts
set a container path,

229
00:13:16,204 --> 00:13:18,293
and mount the host folder separately.

230
00:13:19,617 --> 00:13:21,942
Authentication proved who your code is.

231
00:13:22,262 --> 00:13:25,142
Authorization decides
what it may do, and where.

232
00:13:25,462 --> 00:13:30,476
Google calls this IAM:
a grid of identities, roles, and resources.

233
00:13:30,796 --> 00:13:34,808
Your service account holds
the Gemini user role in your project,

234
00:13:34,808 --> 00:13:36,940
and that green cell does the work.

235
00:13:37,260 --> 00:13:41,612
Storage Admin sits beside it,
granted but never used by this app.

236
00:13:41,850 --> 00:13:43,791
Three requests test three cells.

237
00:13:44,111 --> 00:13:46,760
IAM checks the resource the request names;

238
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:51,365
for an embedding, the client writes
that name from your project variable.

239
00:13:51,685 --> 00:13:54,074
An embedding in your own project is allowed.

240
00:13:54,394 --> 00:13:58,221
The same call
with the script's unedited default project

241
00:13:58,221 --> 00:14:02,458
is refused with a 403,
because your account has no role there.

242
00:14:02,778 --> 00:14:04,634
That is why you edit both scripts.

243
00:14:04,954 --> 00:14:07,066
The third arrow waits for part 2.

244
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:11,420
A DefaultCredentialsError,
or a 401, means no valid token:

245
00:14:11,420 --> 00:14:14,617
check the path, the mount,
and the file name.

246
00:14:14,937 --> 00:14:19,204
A 403 means authentication worked,
but authorization failed.

247
00:14:19,524 --> 00:14:22,737
The cause:
a missing role, the wrong project,

248
00:14:22,737 --> 00:14:24,879
or an API that is not enabled.

249
00:14:25,117 --> 00:14:27,536
That last cause is a small gap:

250
00:14:27,536 --> 00:14:31,282
no README here says
to enable the Vertex AI API.

251
00:14:31,602 --> 00:14:37,256
If you never did, the 403 says so,
and one extra gcloud command fixes it.

252
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:38,844
Something to ponder.

253
00:14:39,164 --> 00:14:40,871
Your key works for embeddings.

254
00:14:41,191 --> 00:14:43,660
Now you paste the instructor's endpoint,

255
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:46,439
which begins with projects
and a long number.

256
00:14:46,759 --> 00:14:49,578
Which part decides whose IAM is checked:

257
00:14:49,578 --> 00:14:51,078
the project variable,

258
00:14:51,078 --> 00:14:54,652
the project inside your key,
or the endpoint path?

259
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:04,500
The path.

260
00:15:04,609 --> 00:15:08,535
Not the key's project:
that only says where the badge was issued.

261
00:15:08,855 --> 00:15:09,956
Not the variable:

262
00:15:09,956 --> 00:15:13,975
the endpoint path already names a project,
and that name wins.

263
00:15:14,295 --> 00:15:18,548
It is someone else's project,
where your account has no role,

264
00:15:18,548 --> 00:15:20,780
so it is a 403; part 2 draws it.

265
00:15:25,067 --> 00:15:27,584
Last stretch of part 1, the API.

266
00:15:27,904 --> 00:15:32,022
In the api-service folder,
make the second project-name edit.

267
00:15:32,342 --> 00:15:33,515
Pause and do it.

268
00:15:34,917 --> 00:15:38,586
Then the README's command,
sh docker-shell.sh.

269
00:15:38,906 --> 00:15:40,058
Pause and run it.

270
00:15:40,378 --> 00:15:43,258
It dies at once: the network already exists.

271
00:15:43,684 --> 00:15:46,863
This is the biggest slip in the repo,
and not your fault.

272
00:15:47,183 --> 00:15:51,311
Line 14 creates the network unconditionally,
and set -e,

273
00:15:51,311 --> 00:15:54,777
on line 4,
stops the script at the first error.

274
00:15:55,097 --> 00:15:57,618
So it fails whenever the network exists,

275
00:15:57,618 --> 00:16:00,580
even right after the README's
own network step.

276
00:16:00,900 --> 00:16:03,392
The fix is the shape you met earlier:

277
00:16:03,392 --> 00:16:06,895
make line 14 the README's
inspect-or-create command.

278
00:16:07,215 --> 00:16:11,353
Pause and make that edit in your editor;
the sed is ours, an extra.

279
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:12,901
Run the script again.

280
00:16:13,221 --> 00:16:17,637
The first build installs 170 pinned packages
from Pipfile.lock,

281
00:16:17,637 --> 00:16:21,563
which took about three minutes
at the time of recording.

282
00:16:21,883 --> 00:16:23,611
You land in the pipenv shell.

283
00:16:23,931 --> 00:16:30,073
Type uvicorn_server,
and the API starts on port 9000,

284
00:16:30,073 --> 00:16:32,507
listening on 0.0.0.0.

285
00:16:32,827 --> 00:16:34,128
Pause and run both.

286
00:16:37,017 --> 00:16:40,522
The server started cleanly
with an empty secrets folder,

287
00:16:40,522 --> 00:16:45,529
because the Google client does not look
for credentials until its first request.

288
00:16:45,849 --> 00:16:47,299
In a second terminal,

289
00:16:47,299 --> 00:16:50,820
an extra curl asks for an embedding
and gets a 500.

290
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:54,387
The server log shows the same
DefaultCredentialsError,

291
00:16:54,387 --> 00:16:56,793
raised while building the bearer header.

292
00:16:57,113 --> 00:17:00,270
A green startup
is not an authenticated startup.

293
00:17:00,850 --> 00:17:07,037
With a real key, the same request returns
a list of 256 numbers, the embedding.

294
00:17:07,357 --> 00:17:11,218
That length must match the loader's,
or the database queries fail.

295
00:17:11,538 --> 00:17:14,461
And the whole chain lights up,
through to a 200.

296
00:17:14,700 --> 00:17:20,353
The README's last step here:
open localhost:9000/docs in your browser.

297
00:17:20,673 --> 00:17:21,761
Pause and look.

298
00:17:22,081 --> 00:17:23,956
Extra curls show the docs page

299
00:17:23,956 --> 00:17:27,458
and the README's five routes,
plus one more at the root.

300
00:17:30,417 --> 00:17:34,798
On your machine, with a real key,
the auth chain is now complete,

301
00:17:34,798 --> 00:17:37,158
and the API answers on your laptop.

302
00:17:37,478 --> 00:17:38,566
Leave it running.

303
00:17:38,886 --> 00:17:40,756
Part 2 asks two questions:

304
00:17:40,756 --> 00:17:44,497
how does a web page find this API,
and who else can?

305
00:17:46,733 --> 00:17:51,021
Part 2 starts with addresses;
every command in this scene is an extra.

306
00:17:51,341 --> 00:17:56,909
The loader dials the database by name:
llm-rag-chromadb:8000.

307
00:17:57,229 --> 00:18:02,050
Your browser will ring localhost:8000,
through a door in the laptop wall.

308
00:18:02,370 --> 00:18:04,162
One server, two addresses.

309
00:18:04,482 --> 00:18:05,912
Why can they not swap?

310
00:18:06,150 --> 00:18:09,649
Each container is its own house,
with its own localhost.

311
00:18:09,969 --> 00:18:13,403
Inside a container,
localhost means that container.

312
00:18:13,723 --> 00:18:17,414
Names work through Docker DNS,
a phone book on each street.

313
00:18:17,734 --> 00:18:21,894
IP addresses change when containers restart,
but names do not.

314
00:18:22,214 --> 00:18:26,481
This extra inspect shows
the API's IP address on its street.

315
00:18:26,801 --> 00:18:31,430
Yours will differ, which is the point:
dial names, not IP addresses.

316
00:18:31,750 --> 00:18:35,485
And your laptop is not on any street,
so it has no phone book:

317
00:18:35,485 --> 00:18:37,894
that name means nothing to your browser.

318
00:18:38,133 --> 00:18:39,178
Now the proof.

319
00:18:39,498 --> 00:18:43,744
From inside the API container,
ask for the database by name.

320
00:18:44,064 --> 00:18:46,666
The answer: Name or service not known.

321
00:18:46,986 --> 00:18:50,058
The API's street has no entry for that name.

322
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:51,644
Something to ponder.

323
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:55,676
Suppose you want the API itself
to query the database.

324
00:18:55,996 --> 00:18:57,406
What address would it use,

325
00:18:57,406 --> 00:19:00,497
and what must change on the map
for that name to resolve?

326
00:19:09,333 --> 00:19:12,213
Put the database
on the API's street as well,

327
00:19:12,213 --> 00:19:14,581
with an extra docker network connect,

328
00:19:14,581 --> 00:19:17,653
and the same name answers
with Chroma's version.

329
00:19:17,973 --> 00:19:20,405
Docker DNS works on a shared street.

330
00:19:20,725 --> 00:19:24,181
And localhost:8000, from inside the API?

331
00:19:24,501 --> 00:19:25,909
Connection refused.

332
00:19:26,229 --> 00:19:29,813
Inside the API, nothing listens on 8000.

333
00:19:30,050 --> 00:19:32,951
Then disconnect it:
that was only an experiment.

334
00:19:33,271 --> 00:19:37,709
Nothing in this app needs that shortcut,
because the browser makes every call.

335
00:19:38,029 --> 00:19:42,367
One small slip: the frontend's config
still forwards its docs path

336
00:19:42,367 --> 00:19:45,325
to a container name
that does not exist here.

337
00:19:45,645 --> 00:19:51,404
For the API docs, use localhost:9000/docs,
as the README says.

338
00:19:52,950 --> 00:19:54,413
Leave the API running,

339
00:19:54,413 --> 00:19:57,537
and open a new terminal
in the frontend folder.

340
00:19:57,857 --> 00:20:00,182
Run its shell script; pause and do it.

341
00:20:00,502 --> 00:20:04,926
The image holds just Node and a shell,
so you run npm yourself;

342
00:20:04,926 --> 00:20:07,243
the hash sign means you are root.

343
00:20:07,483 --> 00:20:09,084
Then npm install:

344
00:20:09,084 --> 00:20:15,206
773 packages at the time of recording,
plus deprecation warnings.

345
00:20:15,526 --> 00:20:17,789
They land in your folder through the mount,

346
00:20:17,789 --> 00:20:21,051
so our capture could step out
and back in without losing them.

347
00:20:21,371 --> 00:20:23,035
Then npm run dev.

348
00:20:23,355 --> 00:20:29,059
The Next.js server says ready,
version 14.2.4 at the time of recording,

349
00:20:29,059 --> 00:20:34,683
and prints the line that fools everyone:
Local: http://localhost:3000.

350
00:20:35,003 --> 00:20:36,305
Pause and run both.

351
00:20:36,550 --> 00:20:39,153
In a second terminal, three extra checks.

352
00:20:39,473 --> 00:20:43,825
First, predict:
will localhost:3000 answer from your laptop?

353
00:20:44,145 --> 00:20:45,873
No, it fails to connect.

354
00:20:46,193 --> 00:20:50,374
From your laptop,
localhost:3200 answers with the RAG app.

355
00:20:50,694 --> 00:20:55,707
And from inside the container,
localhost:3000 answers with the same page.

356
00:20:56,027 --> 00:21:01,318
Now open localhost:3200 in your browser;
the README suggests Chrome.

357
00:21:01,638 --> 00:21:02,726
Pause and look.

358
00:21:02,967 --> 00:21:04,204
That is the tunnel.

359
00:21:04,524 --> 00:21:13,591
The flag -p 3200:3000 means host port
3200 forwards to container port 3000.

360
00:21:13,911 --> 00:21:17,935
The Next server listens
on 3000 inside its own house,

361
00:21:17,935 --> 00:21:20,972
and your browser knocks on 3200 outside.

362
00:21:21,292 --> 00:21:23,084
One server, two addresses.

363
00:21:23,404 --> 00:21:26,177
An extra docker ps shows the same door.

364
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:29,013
Port numbers come in three bands.

365
00:21:29,333 --> 00:21:33,451
The low ones belong to the system,
like 80 and 443.

366
00:21:33,771 --> 00:21:39,979
Pick yours from the middle band:
3000, 3200, 8000, and 9000.

367
00:21:40,299 --> 00:21:43,669
The top band holds throw-away ports
for outgoing calls.

368
00:21:43,917 --> 00:21:49,826
0.0.0.0 has now appeared twice,
as two different facts.

369
00:21:50,146 --> 00:21:54,176
Inside the container,
the tunnel arrives at the street side,

370
00:21:54,176 --> 00:21:58,274
not at the loopback,
so the API server listens on every side.

371
00:21:58,594 --> 00:22:01,566
Docker publishes
on every side of your laptop,

372
00:22:01,566 --> 00:22:03,117
unless you add a prefix.

373
00:22:03,437 --> 00:22:08,016
The ports lecture wrote 127.0.0.1
before its port,

374
00:22:08,016 --> 00:22:10,306
but this app writes none.

375
00:22:10,626 --> 00:22:15,042
So these doors face your Wi-Fi too,
and anyone on that network can knock.

376
00:22:15,362 --> 00:22:21,335
Adding 127.0.0.1
before the host port closes that side.

377
00:22:22,983 --> 00:22:27,099
This setting, baked
into the JavaScript your browser downloads,

378
00:22:27,099 --> 00:22:29,255
gives the page the API's address.

379
00:22:29,575 --> 00:22:35,335
So it must be one your browser can reach:
localhost:9000, never a container name.

380
00:22:35,583 --> 00:22:39,743
Step 1 of 5, text chunking, runs
in your browser, with no bill.

381
00:22:40,063 --> 00:22:44,500
Step 2, the database UI,
connects to localhost:8000.

382
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:46,186
Pause and try both.

383
00:22:46,506 --> 00:22:50,196
Our extra listing is empty;
yours shows two collections.

384
00:22:50,433 --> 00:22:53,723
Step 3, the chat, has a gold tag:
one embedding

385
00:22:53,723 --> 00:22:56,172
and one Gemini answer per question.

386
00:22:56,492 --> 00:22:58,817
Pause, pick a collection, and ask one.

387
00:22:59,137 --> 00:23:01,356
On your machine, it makes three calls.

388
00:23:01,676 --> 00:23:06,646
Call one: the API asks Google,
and 256 numbers come back.

389
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:10,964
Call two asks the database
for the 10 nearest chunks.

390
00:23:11,284 --> 00:23:14,458
An origin is scheme, host and port,

391
00:23:14,458 --> 00:23:18,900
so a page
from 3200 calling 8000 crosses origins.

392
00:23:19,150 --> 00:23:23,971
By default, a browser will not let
a page see replies from another origin.

393
00:23:24,291 --> 00:23:26,430
A server can allow named origins;

394
00:23:26,430 --> 00:23:31,289
that is CORS, and it keeps other websites
from reading your local services.

395
00:23:31,609 --> 00:23:35,342
So the browser first sends a preflight
with the page's origin.

396
00:23:35,662 --> 00:23:38,139
The database checks its guest list,

397
00:23:38,139 --> 00:23:43,022
the course's site and localhost:3200,
and returns a pass, or nothing.

398
00:23:43,342 --> 00:23:46,520
Your browser is the bouncer:
no pass, no query.

399
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:50,727
Call three sends the question

400
00:23:50,727 --> 00:23:54,048
and ten chunks to the API,
and the answer returns.

401
00:23:54,368 --> 00:23:57,333
An extra curl writes its own Origin header.

402
00:23:57,653 --> 00:24:02,495
Both preflights get 200 OK,
but only 3200 gets a pass;

403
00:24:02,495 --> 00:24:05,632
to a browser, no pass is a refusal.

404
00:24:08,166 --> 00:24:09,510
Something to ponder.

405
00:24:09,830 --> 00:24:13,094
You republish the frontend on port 3300.

406
00:24:13,414 --> 00:24:16,230
Which of the three calls fails,
and does curl care?

407
00:24:25,066 --> 00:24:28,437
Call one works: the API allows any origin.

408
00:24:28,757 --> 00:24:30,911
Call two never leaves the browser.

409
00:24:31,231 --> 00:24:33,813
Call three waits for it,
so it is never sent.

410
00:24:34,133 --> 00:24:35,669
And curl ignores it all.

411
00:24:35,989 --> 00:24:38,152
CORS is enforced by the browser,

412
00:24:38,152 --> 00:24:42,005
configured by the server,
and keyed on the page's origin.

413
00:24:42,325 --> 00:24:49,985
Even 127.0.0.1:3200
is a different origin from localhost:3200,

414
00:24:49,985 --> 00:24:53,568
so open the app at localhost.

415
00:24:53,816 --> 00:24:58,168
Look for an answer and ten references,
with book, author and distance.

416
00:24:58,488 --> 00:25:02,049
Last thought:
CORS decides which page may see a reply;

417
00:25:02,049 --> 00:25:04,291
nothing here decides who may call.

418
00:25:04,611 --> 00:25:06,411
An unprotected AI endpoint,

419
00:25:06,411 --> 00:25:09,411
spending your key,
is running on your laptop.

420
00:25:10,950 --> 00:25:16,091
Step 4 is the cheese expert agent:
the chat's three calls, with one twist.

421
00:25:16,411 --> 00:25:21,851
Gemini first picks a retrieval tool:
search by author, or search by content.

422
00:25:22,171 --> 00:25:25,350
The API turns the search text
into an embedding.

423
00:25:25,670 --> 00:25:28,245
Then your browser queries the database,

424
00:25:28,245 --> 00:25:31,217
filtered by author
if Gemini chose that tool.

425
00:25:31,537 --> 00:25:33,741
The API loops up to five times,

426
00:25:33,741 --> 00:25:37,297
and Gemini may call a fun
fact tool on the server.

427
00:25:37,617 --> 00:25:41,090
A gold tag:
one agent question costs one embedding

428
00:25:41,090 --> 00:25:43,590
and three to seven generation calls.

429
00:25:43,910 --> 00:25:46,470
Pause, pick a collection,
and ask it something.

430
00:25:46,717 --> 00:25:50,599
Look for an answer ending
in a highlighted fun fact paragraph.

431
00:25:50,833 --> 00:25:53,436
Step 5 is the fine-tuned cheese model.

432
00:25:53,756 --> 00:25:56,626
The README says to swap
in your own endpoint,

433
00:25:56,626 --> 00:25:59,814
the one your fine-tuning run
printed in lecture 8.

434
00:26:00,134 --> 00:26:01,606
Pause and paste yours.

435
00:26:01,926 --> 00:26:06,961
The server reloads by itself,
because this file sits under the api folder.

436
00:26:07,281 --> 00:26:10,680
Its neighbor,
the agent tools file, is not watched;

437
00:26:10,680 --> 00:26:14,279
for that one,
press Ctrl-C and start the server again.

438
00:26:14,517 --> 00:26:20,128
Then open localhost:3200/finetunechat
and ask the same question.

439
00:26:20,448 --> 00:26:22,688
Here the part 1 puzzle gets its answer.

440
00:26:23,008 --> 00:26:24,198
With the default,

441
00:26:24,198 --> 00:26:28,050
the endpoint path points
into the instructor's project,

442
00:26:28,050 --> 00:26:32,885
where your account has no role,
so expect a 403, or a not-found error.

443
00:26:33,205 --> 00:26:35,771
This call also uses a second client,

444
00:26:35,771 --> 00:26:39,264
in the us location,
because tuned models need it.

445
00:26:39,584 --> 00:26:40,736
A final gold tag:

446
00:26:40,736 --> 00:26:45,685
calls to a tuned model are billed
to whichever project owns the endpoint.

447
00:26:46,005 --> 00:26:51,808
Pause and try it; without your own endpoint,
expect a 403, or a not-found error.

448
00:26:52,128 --> 00:26:57,951
If your own endpoint returns 404 instead,
see the base URL note in the study guide.

449
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,496
Time to shut down, and see what survives.

450
00:27:01,816 --> 00:27:06,723
In the API and frontend terminals,
press Ctrl-C, then type exit.

451
00:27:07,043 --> 00:27:08,216
Pause and do it.

452
00:27:08,536 --> 00:27:11,127
Both containers ran with --rm,

453
00:27:11,127 --> 00:27:15,533
so an extra docker ps shows
only the database left.

454
00:27:15,767 --> 00:27:20,674
Now the README's stop command,
docker compose down, inside llm-rag.

455
00:27:20,994 --> 00:27:22,146
Pause and run it.

456
00:27:22,466 --> 00:27:25,026
The database and its network are removed.

457
00:27:25,346 --> 00:27:27,721
Two warnings say variables are not set,

458
00:27:27,721 --> 00:27:30,462
because we ran this outside
the shell script,

459
00:27:30,462 --> 00:27:33,324
and Compose fills those blanks
from your shell.

460
00:27:33,644 --> 00:27:36,994
That is the mechanism from part 1,
caught in the wild.

461
00:27:37,314 --> 00:27:41,964
Something to ponder: the database is gone,
but are your embeddings gone too?

462
00:27:42,284 --> 00:27:43,841
And what would make them gone?

463
00:27:50,683 --> 00:27:55,543
No. An extra listing shows
the database file still on disk,

464
00:27:55,543 --> 00:28:00,006
in a folder on your laptop,
mounted into the container at /data.

465
00:28:00,326 --> 00:28:03,206
Containers come and go;
mounted folders stay.

466
00:28:03,526 --> 00:28:07,750
An extra restart brings the database back
over that same folder.

467
00:28:08,070 --> 00:28:12,166
Deleting the docker-volumes folder
is what loses your embeddings.

468
00:28:12,486 --> 00:28:14,364
A fresh load rebuilds cleanly,

469
00:28:14,364 --> 00:28:18,246
because the loader deletes
and then recreates each collection.

470
00:28:18,483 --> 00:28:22,384
The cloud version of a mounted folder
is a storage bucket,

471
00:28:22,384 --> 00:28:25,075
a folder any authorized machine can see.

472
00:28:25,395 --> 00:28:27,784
Its folders are only name prefixes.

473
00:28:28,104 --> 00:28:32,518
The storage client takes no key path,
so ADC finds the same key,

474
00:28:32,518 --> 00:28:36,105
and a storage role allows it
to write to the bucket.

475
00:28:36,350 --> 00:28:40,943
This app never calls a bucket,
which is why Storage Admin sat gray;

476
00:28:40,943 --> 00:28:42,451
next lecture uses one.

477
00:28:42,771 --> 00:28:44,457
The lecture gives four rules.

478
00:28:44,777 --> 00:28:47,251
Grant object admin, not full admin.

479
00:28:47,571 --> 00:28:49,043
Keep buckets private.

480
00:28:49,363 --> 00:28:51,539
Prefix their globally unique names.

481
00:28:51,859 --> 00:28:56,489
And delete what you no longer need,
because storage and downloads cost money.

482
00:28:57,533 --> 00:29:01,117
If you tried the restart,
run docker compose down once more.

483
00:29:01,437 --> 00:29:04,253
An extra command removes the shared network.

484
00:29:04,573 --> 00:29:06,557
Pause, and leave your machine clean.

485
00:29:06,800 --> 00:29:09,040
This lecture started nothing in the cloud.

486
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:13,243
What stays behind is the key,
and a leaked key can spend your money.

487
00:29:13,563 --> 00:29:16,784
Delete keys you no longer use,
and their local files.

488
00:29:17,104 --> 00:29:20,485
If one reaches GitHub,
delete it in IAM at once;

489
00:29:20,485 --> 00:29:24,266
last lecture's private key hook
would catch it first,

490
00:29:24,266 --> 00:29:25,978
once added to your repo.

491
00:29:26,216 --> 00:29:28,157
The whole lecture, on one map.

492
00:29:28,477 --> 00:29:33,085
Your code's badge and key buy
a one-hour ticket at Google's token desk.

493
00:29:33,405 --> 00:29:36,712
One IAM cell lets that badge call Gemini.

494
00:29:37,032 --> 00:29:39,400
A mount carries the key to /secrets.

495
00:29:39,720 --> 00:29:44,065
Containers dial names;
your browser dials localhost,

496
00:29:44,065 --> 00:29:48,744
through doors like 3200 to 3000,
open to your Wi-Fi too.

497
00:29:49,064 --> 00:29:52,478
The browser stitches three calls
past the CORS bouncer,

498
00:29:52,478 --> 00:29:55,208
and the vectors survive in a mounted folder.

499
00:29:56,350 --> 00:29:58,547
When something breaks, find its layer.

500
00:29:58,867 --> 00:30:01,491
A credentials error: the path or the mount.

501
00:30:01,811 --> 00:30:04,179
A 401: no valid token.

502
00:30:04,499 --> 00:30:07,678
A 403: role, project, or API.

503
00:30:07,998 --> 00:30:09,918
A CORS line: the origin.

504
00:30:10,238 --> 00:30:12,009
A taken port: the doors.

505
00:30:12,329 --> 00:30:14,483
A dying script: line 14.

506
00:30:14,733 --> 00:30:16,738
5 questions to test yourself.

507
00:30:17,058 --> 00:30:21,410
One: the three places ADC looks;
which does this tutorial fill?

508
00:30:21,730 --> 00:30:24,013
Two: why /secrets, not your Mac?

509
00:30:24,333 --> 00:30:28,834
Three: why can the loader's name
and your browser's localhost not swap?

510
00:30:29,154 --> 00:30:35,106
Four: on port 3300, which call breaks first,
and why does the API survive?

511
00:30:35,426 --> 00:30:39,565
Five: a 403 has three causes;
how do you tell them apart?

512
00:30:39,800 --> 00:30:43,491
Answers are in the exercises,
commands in the study guide.

513
00:30:43,811 --> 00:30:48,973
Next lecture: a new service account,
and real buckets with their own CORS list.

